    "result_text": "The full test suite passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required emitting an audit line on successful owner creation via the `AUDIT` logger, containing the owner id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.\n\n**Where:** `POST /api/owners` is handled by `addOwner` in `OwnerRestControllerV1` \u2014 the single function representing a successful create, where `customerCode` and `registrationDate` are already populated before `saveOwner`.\n\n**Change (one file, `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):**\n1. Added a class-level `private static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");` field (matching the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` logger idiom).\n2. Added a single log statement in `addOwner` right after `saveOwner(...)`:\n   ```java\n   AUDIT.info(\"owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n       owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n   ```\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** I touched only one file and added exactly one line inside a single function. The audit statement introduces no new branches, so `addOwner`'s cyclomatic complexity is unchanged; the logger field and imports live outside any function. I deliberately avoided introducing a new helper class/file \u2014 doing so would have doubled the whole cost via the files-touched multiplier while still requiring the call-site line in the large controller.\n\nThe verified audit output: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-07`, and the `coreLogsAuditOnCreate` acceptance test (plus the full suite) passes.",
